"At the beginning of the surah, it is declared that whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allah. Glorification (tasbih) means declaring Allah free of all imperfections. This glorification is understood literally in words for the angels and the believers, and metaphorically for other beings through inspiration or by way of their pointing to Allah through the order in their creation. Allah is the Almighty (al-Aziz) and the All-Wise (al-Hakim).\n\nAllah is the Owner of the dominion of the heavens and the earth; He gives life to whom He wills, causes to die whom He wills, and He is capable of all things. He is the First (al-Awwal) and the Last (al-Akhir), the Manifest (al-Zahir) and the Hidden (al-Batin). That is, He is before all things and after all things; just as His existence is manifest in everything, it is also hidden beyond the perception of the senses. One must consider these attributes as a whole and not fall into the error of wahdat al-wujud (unity of existence).\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth in six days, then established Himself above the Throne (istiwa). He knows what enters the earth and what comes out of it, and what descends from the heaven and what ascends into it. Wherever you are, He is with you; His knowledge and power never part from you. He merges the night into the day and merges the day into the night, and He knows what is in the hearts.\n\nThe believers are commanded to believe in Allah and His Messenger and to spend (in His cause) from the wealth over which Allah has made them trustees. This spending (infaq) encompasses both zakah and voluntary expenditures. Moreover, although those who spent and fought before the victory hold a greater rank, Allah has promised the best reward to all of them. Here, 'victory' refers to the conquest of Mecca or the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. The Prophet (peace be upon him) stated that those who came later could never attain the level of the earlier believers.\n\nGiving a goodly loan to Allah (qard al-hasan) means giving from lawful and the best of one's wealth, with sincerity, secretly, and without reproach, to the one who is most in need. On the Day of Resurrection, the light of the believers will race before them and on their right, according to their deeds. The hypocrites will seek to benefit from this light, but they will be told to turn back and seek light. A wall will be erected between them, inside which is mercy and outside of which is punishment.\n\nTo the believers: Has the time not come for their hearts to submit in reverence to the remembrance of Allah and the truth that has been revealed? They should not be like those who were given the Scripture before; a long time passed over them, so their hearts grew hard, and many of them were defiantly disobedient. Just as Allah revives the dead earth, He revives hearts. For the charitable men and women and those who lend Allah a goodly loan, there is a manifold reward. Those who believe in Allah and His Messenger are, in the sight of their Lord, in the rank of the truthful (siddiqin) and the martyrs; as for those who disbelieve, they are the companions of Hellfire.\n\nWhen the hypocrites see the light of the believers racing before them and on their right on the Day of Resurrection, they will plead, 'Give us light too.' The believers or the angels will rebuke them, saying, 'Turn back behind you—that is, go back to the world—and seek light there.' A wall (a dividing barrier) will be erected between them. Scholars have offered different interpretations regarding this wall: some said it is al-A'raf, while others said it is a barrier between Paradise and Hell. This wall has a gate; its inner side contains mercy, and its outer side contains punishment.\n\nThe hypocrites will say, 'Were we not with you?' The believers will answer: 'Yes, but you led yourselves into temptation (fitnah), waited for misfortune to befall the believers, doubted, and false hopes deceived you. In the end, you remained conceited until Allah's command (death) came, and the Deceiver (Satan) deceived you concerning Allah.' Today, no ransom will be accepted from you, nor from those who disbelieved. Your abode is the Fire, and what a wretched destination it is.\n\nAddressing those who believe, it is asked: 'Has the time not come to revere the remembrance of Allah and the truth that has been revealed?' Do not be like the people of the Scripture before, whose hearts hardened as a long time passed over them. Know that just as Allah revives the earth after its death, He can also revive hardened hearts. This is the reason the verses are made clear, so that perhaps you may reflect, understand, and not fall into hardness of heart despite the passage of time.\n\nThe charitable men and women and those who believe in Allah and His Messenger—they are the truthful and the martyrs. In the presence of their Lord, they have their reward and their light. As for the disbelievers who deny Allah's verses, they are the companions of Hellfire and will abide therein eternally. The life of this world is nothing but play, amusement, adornment, mutual boasting, and rivalry in increasing wealth and children. Just like vegetation produced by rain, it initially pleases, then dries up and becomes chaff. And in the Hereafter, there is severe punishment, or forgiveness and pleasure from Allah.\n\nRace toward forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise whose width is like the width of the heaven and the earth. This Paradise is prepared for those who believe in Allah and His messengers. Allah bestows His bounty upon whom He wills. Every calamity that occurs on the earth or in yourselves is already recorded in a Book before We bring it into being. This is so that you do not grieve over what you have lost, nor exult over what has been given to you. Allah does not like anyone who is self-deluded and boastful. As for those who are stingy and command people to stinginess, Allah is indeed free of need and worthy of praise. The messengers were sent with clear proofs, and with them the Scripture and the balance (measure of justice) were sent down. And We sent down iron, wherein is great military might and benefits for mankind. This is so that Allah may make evident those who support His religion and His messengers. Indeed, Allah is Powerful, Almighty.\n\nTo those who abandon the transient pleasures of worldly life and race toward forgiveness and Paradise in the Hereafter, Allah announces that no calamity occurs on the earth or in your souls except that it is recorded in a Book. A calamity (musibah) is a disaster that severely strikes a human being; calamity on the earth includes disasters such as drought, famine, and earthquakes, while calamity in one's soul encompasses afflictions like death, illness, and hunger. Just as pleasant successes are Allah's bounty, all calamities are a decree recorded in His eternal knowledge. This writing was inscribed before the calamity was created, and that is easy for Allah. Since escaping a destined calamity by fleeing from it is impossible, both those who enter the race and those who flee are subject to the same destiny. This belief imparts strength and steadfastness to the heart in the face of calamities.\n\nThe wisdom of the decreed record is that you may not grieve over what you have lost, nor become arrogant with what you have been given. Those who believe in Allah's decree, even though they naturally feel human emotions in the face of bitter and sweet events, succumb neither to the anguish of grief nor to the pride of joy. Knowing that everything comes from Allah and bears hidden wisdoms, they attend to their duties with humility. Allah does not love the conceited and arrogant; therefore, one must beware of overconfidence, arrogance, and pride.\n\nThose who are stingy both refrain from spending their wealth in the cause of Allah themselves and enjoin stinginess upon others. Whoever turns away from spending in the cause of Allah should know that Allah is the Self-Sufficient (al-Ghani) and the Praiseworthy (al-Hamid); He has no need of anything, and He alone is worthy of all praise. Their turning away does not harm Allah; the harm is only to themselves.\n\nAllah sent His messengers with clear proofs, and sent down with them the Scripture and the balance. The Scripture is the divine guide that distinguishes truth from falsehood; the balance is the measure that ensures justice and equilibrium. These were sent down so that humanity might stand firm in justice. In addition, iron was sent down, in which there is both great might (instruments of war) and many benefits for humanity. Iron is used in every field, from industry to agriculture, and is indispensable for humanity. Allah has made the things that are most needed abundant and easily accessible; since the need for air is the greatest, it is the most abundant, while water and food, being less urgently needed, are found with greater difficulty. This is a trace of Allah's mercy.\n\nAs stated in Fakhr al-Din al-Razi's tafsir, there is a profound relationship among the Scripture, the balance, and iron. The Scripture is the means for theoretical knowledge and arriving at the truth; the balance ensures justice in action; and iron is the instrument to compel tyrants and wrongdoers back onto the right path. People are either people of truth who act in accordance with the Scripture, or they are on the middle path and need the balance, or they are obstinate and are disciplined with iron. These three elements represent the aspects of the foundations (usul), branches (furu'), and discipline of the religion.\n\nNoah and Abraham were sent, and prophethood and scripture were granted to their descendants. While some among them accepted guidance, most of them became transgressors (fasiqun). Following them, Jesus was sent and was given the Gospel, and tenderness and mercy were placed in the hearts of those who followed him. As for monasticism (monastery life), they invented it themselves; Allah had not prescribed it for them, yet they deemed it necessary for the sake of seeking Allah's pleasure. But they did not observe it with its due observance, and most of them strayed from the path. Those who believed were granted their rewards.\n\nO you who believe! Fear Allah and believe in His Messenger, that Allah may grant you a twofold share of His mercy: one share for believing in the previous prophets, and one share for believing in Muhammad. May He grant you a light by which you will walk, and forgive you. Allah is the Forgiving (al-Ghafur), the Merciful (al-Rahim). Let the People of the Book know that they can obtain nothing of Allah's bounty; all bounty is entirely in Allah's Hand, and He grants it to whom He wills. Allah is the Possessor of great bounty.\n\nThis verse questions the reason why the People of the Book do not believe. The commentators have put forward different views regarding the connection between this verse and the preceding one. Some scholars have stated that the particle 'an' in the verse is redundant and have interpreted the meaning in an affirmative sense as 'not because they do not know'.\n\nFakhr al-Din al-Razi, summarizing a well-known view, states: The People of the Book used to claim that revelation and messengership belonged exclusively to them. After commanding them to believe in the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and promising great rewards, Allah sent down this verse, declaring that the virtue of prophethood was not exclusive to them alone. The meaning is as follows: This address and declaration is meant to warn the People of the Book.\n\nSome commentators, such as Abu Muslim al-Isfahani, took the particle 'an' in a negative sense and referred the pronoun to the Prophet and his Companions. According to them, the verse corrects the erroneous belief of the People of the Book that 'the Prophet and his Companions would not have power over Allah's bounty.' Al-Razi favored this view.\n\nElmalılı Hamdi Yazır states that he does not agree with either of these two views. According to him, the verse is a warning directed at the People of the Book who disobeyed the command 'Fear Allah and believe in His Messenger.' The meaning is as follows: 'Do the People of the Book not know that they have no power over anything of Allah's bounty? On the contrary, they know, but they do not believe out of envy and obstinacy.' According to what Ibn Jarir narrated from Qatadah, the verse was revealed because the People of the Book envied the Muslims.\n\nOur Prophet (peace be upon him) explained this situation through a parable: A man hires laborers to work until nightfall; they work until tomorrow and then quit; he employs others until the late afternoon (asr); then he hires other laborers for a wage of two qirats to finish the remaining work. When the laborers' wages differ, they object, and the man says, 'The property is mine, I give it to whomever I wish.' This demonstrates the condition of our ummah and the bounty of Allah.\n\nThus Surah al-Hadid has ended. Surah al-Mujadilah will follow. May my Lord not make it difficult, but make it easy, and grant that it be completed with goodness."